Okay, I've been iterating on the visual style of the scene: adjusting material colors, emissive properties, tweaking light colors, and even changing the hemisphere light's ground color. After each set of changes I reload the preview and take a snapshot to see the effect. The snapshots (though not displayed here) have been confirming that the scene looks brighter and more vivid, which is what the user wants.

Now I want to push the visual fidelity further. Right now the blob objects (the large icosahedron “blob” that probably represents some terrain or creature) are generated with `new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.9, 1)`. A low subdivision level (detail = 1) means relatively few vertices, which limits how much I can deform the geometry in the “crumple the blob” section that follows. The code currently does:

```
const blobGeo = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.9, 1);
{ // crumple the blob
  const p = blobGeo.attributes.position;
  // ... modify positions ...
}
```

If I increase the subdivision level (the second argument) to something higher, I’ll get more vertices to work with, but the geometry will also contain many duplicate vertices because the icosahedron is built from triangles that share edges. When I later modify the vertex positions, those duplicate vertices will move independently, causing visual artifacts like seams or non‑smooth deformation.

Three.js provides a utility `mergeVertices` (from `three/addons/utils/BufferGeometryUtils.js`) that removes duplicate vertices, merging them into single shared vertices while preserving the overall shape. I already imported `mergeVertices` a few lines earlier:

```
import { mergeVertices } from 'three/addons/utils/BufferGeometryUtils.js';
```

So the next logical step is to apply `mergeVertices` to the blob geometry right after creation, before the crumpling loop. I’ll also increase the subdivision level from 1 to 2 to give a finer mesh, which will make the crumpling look smoother. The new line should replace the original geometry creation line, preserving the surrounding block comment.

Thus I will edit the file `index.html`, targeting the exact snippet that defines `blobGeo`. I’ll replace the two‑line block:

```
  const blobGeo = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.9, 1);
  { // crumple the blob
    const p = blobGeo.attributes.position;
```

with a version that merges vertices and uses a higher detail level:

```
  const blobGeo = mergeVertices(new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.9, 2));
  { // crumple the blob
    const p = blobGeo.attributes.position;
```

I’ll keep `replace_all` set to false because I only want to replace this specific occurrence, not any other similar strings. This change should improve the deformation quality of the blob without breaking anything else.

